On Wed, 17 Dec 2008, Derek Ray wrote:

> Quest leaders are far too obnoxious in Spork to sic on an unsuspecting
> player.  Quest nemeses are far too weak.  (remember, they're targeted
> for XL14/15).

I know I sound like a broken record, but here's something connecting to 
your statement. In my opinion, it's a non-controversial and fitting 
feature which also addresses a basic flaw in Nethack's story.

The flaw:
In two places, Nethack tells you that Home:1 is under siege and urgent 
action is needed. Firstly when entering the dungeon level containing the 
portal (the cryptic message), secondly when jumping through the portal 
(that page of text).
Now the fact is that there is no urgency. The quest leader will happily 
wait another 100000 turns until you arrive. For some classes, postponing 
the quest as far as possible is generally advised. Another flaw is that 
the Quest text describes a battle, but attackers and defenders will 
peacefully walk by each other. I recall that I found both facts disturbing 
when playing Nethack.

My proposal:
(1) Apply some sort of the grudge patch to Home:1. It is not ideal, as it 
only causes combat for adjacent beings. So additional AI would be helpful.

(2) Track the player's effort in rescuing the Quest leader and his 
friends. This could be done by counting (and weighting properly)
* number of turns between getting the message and entering portal,
* number of turns spent on Home:1 (until you meet the Leader) [1],
* number of casualties on your faction,
* number of enemies killed.
[1] When evaluating quickness on Home:1, the numbers must depend on the 
class in question. For example, the Ranger quest takes notoriously long to 
finish (and is hard), many others are much quicker.

(3) When you finally reach the leader, give a message about performance. 
This reinstates consistency. Furthermore, hand out a prize for 
good performance. Like for Sokoban, two prizes seem nice. Below is a 
suggestion for once prize.

(4) Gear improvement: depending on performance, determine a number N 
between 0 and 8. Then enchant gear (worn armour, wielded weapon) N times, 
targets chosen randomly. Items do not vapourise.

This should create several choices:
(a) Do you want to go for the reward? This might contain a risk, and the 
loss of resources.
(b) If you do, there's a lot you can do tactically: stinking cloud, wands, 
jumping etc. all come into play. I think it's a fun minigame, in 
particular due to the change of pace.
(c) If you manage to beat Home:1 in a reasonable manner (so that you can 
expect a reward), there's the choice of what to wear: if you use full, 
lightly enchanted gear, you'll get most out of the enchantments, in 
absolute number of pluses. If you wear/wield a single, highly enchanted 
item, you may end up with a +9 item -- something potentially of endgame 
quality.
(d) Note that nothing changes for players opting to ignore all of this.

This isn't too hard to code, my sister did it several years ago. 
The code is gone, don't ask. We've both moved to Crawl meantime.

Additional notes:
* What if the leader dies due to the grudge? We chose to just open the 
staircase to Home:2. It would be better if something bad happened, at 
least for flavour.
* It might be an option to have Home:1 change depending on how late you 
enter. This could be visuals only: buildings (more and more) broken etc. 
Could also add more enemies, but that takes some balancing with respect to 
how you evaluate performance.

Just something on topic, I hope
David


